From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 09:38:52 -0400
Subject: feat: ad and tracker blocking, off by default (S-88)

Request blocking built on Chromium's own subresource filter, behind
ADR 0030 (Proposed). docs/features/blocker.md rows:

- Bk1: one switch, stedding.blocker.enabled in local state, off. While
  it is on, a Stedding throttle decides each top-level page's
  activation through SubresourceFilterObserverManager; while off,
  Chromium's ad-tagging dry run is left as it is.
- Bk2, Bk3, Bk12: EasyList and EasyPrivacy are fetched from easylist.to
  only while blocking is on, each when its own Expires line says, with
  a conditional request carrying no cookies, cache or query; converted
  in the browser by Chromium's EasyList parser into one ruleset of URL
  rules handed to RulesetService, with a LICENSE file naming each list.
  A download that is not a filter list keeps the previous ruleset; a
  version the service never indexed is made again under a new salt.
- Bk4: Google's Subresource Filter Rules component is not registered.
- Bk5, Bk6, Bk10: the switch for a site is the ADS content setting
  (user entries only, the default is not read), from a shield in the
  address row, command bar rows and Settings; a private window's choice
  stays in its session.
- Bk7: while Stedding decides, the throttle manager shows no "ads
  blocked" icon and triggers no ads intervention, so no ADS_DATA is
  written for the sites visited.
- Bk8: sign-in steps are let through. Bk9: Settings rows. Bk11: the
  activation is in place before the first window loads a page.

Why: built-in blocking is on the M10 list (S-88); the subresource
filter is BSD code already in every binary, matches in the renderer
from one shared index, and needs one small embedder hook instead of a
second engine in the network path.
Removable when: the throttle manager hunk goes once upstream offers an
embedder hook for page activation; the feature itself stays.
